:: Inklink [config]
(((
Ex 8: a short solo `story`, written for Inquests. A draft for Inkjot v0.6.

Hythe Fen, November 1893. Amos Rudd, keeper of Hythe Lock, drowned on the
night the sluice was opened. The inquest sits in the long room of the
Plough. You are the coroner's clerk, and you write down what is said.

The frame is in the second person, to you. Each testimony is in the
first person, as the witness: you choose what they did that night, and
what they say about it, which is not always the same thing. Some
witnesses are reliable. Some are not.

An Inquest here is the coroner's *suppose*: "Suppose you had gone back
for the lantern. What then?" The witness imagines the other night, and in
imagining it, remembers something. Three of them; spend them where you
think the truth is.

What happened, for the Inker (spoilers): Edmund Garrard of the Grange
paid Ned Fisk, an eel-catcher, to draw the sluice pins and wind up the
paddles at ten, so the flood would go down the lock cut and not across
his eight hundred acres. Tom Rudd, checking the gates for the fifth time
that night, found the pins out and a man by the paddle frame, and ran
home. Amos went out without his lantern. He fought Fisk on the walkway
for the paddle chain and went into the water. Garrard held the light.

The verdict is what the record holds, not what happened.
)))
