:: notice [prologue]
told: second person
--
[quote edict ~ by "J. Fenwick, Coroner for the Isle of Ely"]
**NOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN** that an Inquest will be held at the Plough Inn, Hythe Fen, on *Tuesday the 14th of November, 1893*, at ten o’clock in the forenoon, touching the death of **AMOS RUDD**, Lock-keeper, whose body was found in the Hythe Lock cut on the morning of the 10th instant.
[end]

[after 2s]
The long room of the Plough smells of wet coats and last night’s beer. Twelve men of the parish sit on two benches with their hats on their knees. They have been to view the body in the outhouse, as the law requires, and they have come back quieter.

[after 2s]
You lay out the depositions book, three pens and a knife to mend them. Mr. Fenwick takes the chair by the fire and looks at his watch.

@fenwick (to you) Everything they say, mind. Not what they mean. What they say.

> [[Call the widow->martha]] ~ id call_martha ~ become martha ~ beat opening ~ tone dark

:: martha
told: first person
--
@fenwick Mrs. Rudd. Tell the jury about the night of the ninth.

It had rained three days. The river was up to the second mark on the lock wall by tea, and Amos said the Grange would want the sluice drawn before morning, and the Grange would not get it. *Not while I’m keeper,* he said. He said it to his plate.

Tom was out at the gates. Tom was always out at the gates.

> [[Say that Tom was out at the gates->martha:tom_out]] ~ id martha_truth ~ weighs truth ~ arc martha holds ~ stakes other ~ plants checking ~ choice proactive
> [[Say that Tom was abed->martha:tom_abed]] ~ id martha_shield ~ weighs mercy ~ stakes other ~ predict 40% ~ choice proactive ~ tone tense

:: martha:tom_out
set pins_out: yes
--
@martha Tom was out at the gates. He goes four or five times, on a bad night. He came in at half past nine running, with no cap, and said the pins were out. All four. Amos had his boots on before Tom had his breath back.

@fenwick And the lantern?

@martha It was on the hook. I saw it on the hook after the door shut. I thought, *he’ll come back for it.*

> [[Say that you stayed by the fire->martha:fire]] ~ id stayed ~ beat inciting ~ weighs duty ~ lasting
> [[Suppose she had taken him the lantern->martha:lantern]] ~ inquest for stayed ~ id lantern ~ credit inker ~ reveals the_gig

:: martha:tom_abed
set martha_shielded: yes
--
@martha Tom was abed. He’s a good boy. He was abed.

@fenwick Then who told your husband the pins were out?

I look at my hands. I hadn’t thought of that.

@martha Nobody. Amos went to look. He always went to look.

[note]
The record now says the boy was asleep. Whatever Tom says later, Mr. Fenwick will remember this.

[end]
> [[Step down->after_martha]] ~ id martha_down ~ become clerk

:: martha:fire
--
@martha I stayed by the fire. I sat there with the lantern on its hook and his plate on the table and I listened to the water. At eleven I went to the door. At twelve I went to the lock.

> [[Step down->after_martha]] ~ id martha_down2 ~ become clerk

:: martha:lantern
set gig_heard: yes
learn the_gig
--
@fenwick Suppose you had taken it to him, Mrs. Rudd. The lantern. Suppose you had gone after him down the drove. What would you have seen?

I think about it. I have thought about nothing else for five days, but never out loud.

@martha I’d have gone by the Grange drove. It’s quicker. And I’d have had to stand aside on the bank, because—

I stop.

@martha Because there was a gig on the drove. I heard it. Wheels, and a horse blowing, at ten or a little after, going down toward the lock, not away from it. I didn’t think. I thought it was the doctor.

@fenwick It was not the doctor, Mrs. Rudd. The doctor was at Mepal.

> [[Step down->after_martha]] ~ id martha_down3 ~ become clerk ~ pays off checking

:: after_martha
told: second person
--
You blot the deposition and read it back to her. She signs it with a cross, very small, in the corner, as if she didn’t want to take up room.

[if gig_heard]
You underline *a gig on the drove*. Then you underline it again.

[end]
> [[Call the son->tom]] ~ id call_tom ~ become tom ~ beat turn

:: tom
told: first person
--
@fenwick Thomas Rudd. You are seventeen? Then you’ll speak up.

[if not martha_shielded]
Mother has told them I go out to the gates. I can tell by the way the jury look at me. They’ve heard about me in the village.

[if martha_shielded]
Mother has told them I was abed. I wasn’t. I’m on oath.

[end]
I check the gates. Paddles down, chains slack, pins home, beam latched. In that order, and then again from the start, because if I start in the middle I don’t know that I’ve done it. On a bad night I go five times. Father said I wore a path.

The ninth was a bad night.

> [[Tell them about the checks->tom:checks]] ~ id tom_checks ~ weighs truth ~ arc tom rises ~ plants the_fifth ~ tone warm
> [[Say you checked twice, and leave it->tom:twice]] ~ id tom_hide ~ weighs mercy ~ arc tom falls ~ predict 35%

:: tom:checks
raise tom.count 1
--
I went at seven, and eight, and nine. Each time:

{list checks ~ rotate 2s}

At nine they were all home. I put my hand on each pin. I counted them. I counted them again on the way back up the bank, in my head, and when I got in I couldn’t be sure, so I went out again.

@fenwick (not unkindly) A fourth time.

@tom A fifth, sir. Half past nine was the fifth.

> [[Count them again->tom:again]] ~ id count_again ~ choice inactive ~ tone warm
> [[Tell them about half past nine->tom:half_nine]] ~ id half_nine ~ beat midpoint ~ pays off the_fifth ~ stakes other

:: tom:again
raise tom.count 1
--
{list checks ~ rotate 2s}

{if tom.count == 2 then "Twice" else if tom.count == 3 then "Three times" else "Four times"} through, now, in front of them all. It holds still when you say it in order.

[if tom.count < 4]
@fenwick (waiting) Take your time, Thomas.

[else]
@fenwick (gently) That will do, Thomas. Thank you. Now: half past nine.

[end]
> [[Count them again->tom:again]] ~ id count_more ~ when tom.count < 4 ~ choice inactive
> [[Tell them about half past nine->tom:half_nine]] ~ id half_nine2 ~ pays off the_fifth

:: tom:twice
--
@tom I checked them twice, sir. At seven and at nine. They were home.

@fenwick And after nine?

@tom I was in, sir.

The jury nod. A sensible boy. Two checks is sensible. My hands are shaking and I put them in my pockets.

> [[Step down->after_tom]] ~ id tom_down ~ become clerk

:: tom:half_nine
set pins_out: yes
set man_at_frame: yes
--
@tom At half past nine the pins were out. All four, lying on the beam in a row, like someone had put them down neatly to come back for. And there was a man by the paddle frame. I didn’t see his face. I smelled him. Eel-trap. That sour smell, like a ditch in August.

@fenwick And you did what?

> [[Say you ran home for your father->tom:ran]] ~ id ran ~ weighs duty ~ lasting ~ beat crisis
> [[Suppose he had stayed by the gates->tom:stayed]] ~ inquest for ran ~ id tom_stayed ~ credit inker ~ reveals the_voice

:: tom:ran
--
@tom I ran home. I should have stayed. I should have put the pins back. I couldn’t, sir, I couldn’t touch them with him there, I ran.

@fenwick You did right to fetch your father.

He means it kindly. It is the worst thing anyone has said to me.

> [[Step down->after_tom]] ~ id tom_down2 ~ become clerk

:: tom:stayed
learn the_voice
set fisk_there: yes
set light_on_bank: yes
--
@fenwick Suppose you had stayed, Thomas. Suppose you had stood your ground by the gates. What do you think you would have seen?

I close my eyes. I can see it better with them closed. I always could.

@tom He’d have started on the chain. You can’t wind the paddles quiet, the pawl clicks. And somebody up on the bank held a light for him, a good lamp, not a lantern, with a glass. I saw the light. I forgot I saw it. And he said, the man said, up to the light: *Nearly there, sir.*

@tom It was Ned Fisk’s voice. I’ve bought eels off him. Everybody has.

> [[Step down->after_tom]] ~ id tom_down3 ~ become clerk

:: after_tom
told: second person
--
Tom signs his name in a careful hand, each letter separate, and then looks at it for a long time to be sure it is his.

[if light_on_bank]
*A light on the bank, a lamp with a glass.* There is one house in the parish that keeps a carriage lamp.

[end]
> [[Pass the coroner a note: ask the doctor about the hands->after_tom:note]] ~ id note_hands ~ choice proactive ~ plants hands
> [[Call the surgeon->hesketh]] ~ id call_hesketh ~ become hesketh

:: after_tom:note
raise notes_passed 1
--
You tear a strip from the foot of a page and write on it, *Ask the doctor about the hands*, and fold it twice, and pass it up the table. Mr. Fenwick reads it without looking at you and puts it under his inkstand.

> [[Call the surgeon->hesketh]] ~ id call_hesketh2 ~ become hesketh

:: hesketh
told: first person
--
@fenwick Dr. Hesketh. You examined the body.

I examined it on the morning of the tenth, in the outhouse at the lock. A man of fifty or thereabouts, well nourished. He had been in the water some eight or nine hours. The cause of death was drowning.

There was a contusion above the left ear, two inches long, with a straight edge.

[if notes_passed >= 1]
@fenwick (glancing at a note) And his hands, Doctor?

@hesketh Rope burns across both palms, fresh. He had hold of something that ran through them. A chain would do it.

[end]
> [[Say the beam did it->hesketh:beam]] ~ id beam ~ weighs truth ~ predict 50% ~ tone dark
> [[Say it was a blow->hesketh:blow]] ~ id blow ~ weighs truth ~ arc hesketh falls ~ predict 15%
> [[Say you cannot tell->hesketh:cannot]] ~ id cannot ~ weighs truth ~ arc hesketh holds
> [[Suppose he had looked at another man’s hands->hesketh:other_hands]] ~ inquest ~ id other_hands ~ reveals the_burns

:: hesketh:beam
set a_fall: yes
--
@hesketh The lock beam has a squared edge. A man who slipped on the walkway in the dark might strike it going in. I should think that likely.

> [[Step down->after_hesketh]] ~ id hesketh_down ~ become clerk

:: hesketh:blow
set a_blow: yes
--
@hesketh A blow, I should say. From something with an edge, swung. The beam is not where a man’s head would strike it, going in.

It is more than I said on the morning of the tenth. The clerk was there, and wrote it down. The clerk’s pen stops, and then writes this down too.

> [[Step down->after_hesketh]] ~ id hesketh_down4 ~ become clerk

:: hesketh:cannot
--
@hesketh A squared edge. The beam has one. So has a mattock, or the iron of a paddle winch. I cannot tell the jury which, and I will not guess.

@fenwick Thank you, Doctor. That is the most useful thing anyone has said today.

> [[Step down->after_hesketh]] ~ id hesketh_down2 ~ become clerk

:: hesketh:other_hands
(((
A pending fork with one home (decided 20 Nov, built with MonkeyPaw): the
placeholder passage says everything, and the fork just says `~ inquest`.
MonkeyPaw writes the Suppose, to the brief, within a minute; if nothing
comes in time, this body is what the Linker reads.
)))
pending: by monkeypaw ~ rejoin after_hesketh ~ brief grief ~ timeout 1m
learn the_burns
--
@fenwick Suppose, Doctor, you had been asked to look at another man’s hands that morning. Anyone in this room. What would you have looked for?

@hesketh The same burns. A chain runs both ways. If two men had hold of it, two men would carry the mark for a week.

I look, without meaning to, at the back bench, where the men who are not on the jury stand. Ned Fisk has his hands in his pockets.

> [[Step down->after_hesketh]] ~ id hesketh_down3 ~ become clerk

:: after_hesketh
told: second person
--
The doctor signs with a flourish and pockets his pen before you can offer him yours.

[if notes_passed >= 1; then set amos_at_paddles: yes]
*Rope burns, both palms.* Whatever else happened, Amos Rudd had his hands on the paddle chain when he went in.

[end]
> [[Call Edward Fisk->fisk]] ~ id call_fisk ~ become fisk ~ beat crisis

:: fisk
told: first person
--
@fenwick Edward Fisk. Eel-catcher, of Byall Fen. Where were you on the night of the ninth?

[if man_at_frame and fisk_there]
They’ve all heard the boy. *Eel-trap.* *Nearly there, sir.* Half the room has bought eels off me. The other half owes me for them.

[if not fisk_there]
Nobody has said my name. Nobody needs to. I can feel the back bench looking at my hands.

[end]
> [[Say you were at your traps on Byall Fen->fisk:traps]] ~ id fisk_lie ~ weighs truth ~ arc fisk falls ~ predict 70% ~ tone tense
> [[Say you were at the lock->fisk:lock]] ~ id fisk_truth ~ when man_at_frame ~ weighs truth ~ arc fisk rises ~ stakes self ~ beat climax
> [[Suppose he had refused the shilling->fisk:shilling]] ~ inquest for fisk_lie ~ id shilling ~ credit inker ~ reveals the_shilling ~ lasting

:: fisk:traps
--
@fisk At my traps, sir, on Byall Fen, all night. Two mile off. There’s twenty traps want lifting on a night like that.

@fenwick Can anyone speak to it?

@fisk The eels, sir.

The room laughs, which is what I wanted. The foreman doesn’t.

> [[Step down->after_fisk]] ~ id fisk_down ~ become clerk

:: fisk:lock
set fisk_there: yes
set fisk_confessed: yes
set a_blow: no
retell: "Ned Fisk told the inquest he was on the lock walkway when Amos Rudd went in."
--
@fisk I was at the lock. I pulled the pins and I wound the paddles, because I was paid to. And Amos come along the walkway with no light, shouting, and got hold of the chain, and we had it between us.

@fisk I never hit him. On my mother. He pulled and I pulled and the chain ran and he went off the end. I heard him hit the beam. I heard him go in.

@fenwick And you did what?

@fisk I ran, sir. Same as the boy.

> [[Step down->after_fisk]] ~ id fisk_down2 ~ become clerk

:: fisk:shilling
learn the_shilling
set garrard_ordered: yes
--
@fenwick Suppose you had been offered money for some work that night, Fisk, and had turned it down. Where would you have been?

I turn my cap round in my hands. A shilling. It was a shilling, and a half-crown after, and a promise of the Grange water for my traps.

@fisk Home, sir. If I’d said no to the Squire I’d have been home in my bed.

The room goes quiet in the way a room does when a man has said a name without meaning to.

@fenwick Which Squire, Fisk?

@fisk There’s only the one, sir.

@fenwick Then I will ask you again. Where were you?

> [[Say you were at the lock->fisk:lock]] ~ id fisk_truth2 ~ when man_at_frame ~ pays off hands ~ beat climax
> [[Say you were at your traps on Byall Fen->fisk:traps]] ~ id fisk_lie2 ~ tone tense

:: after_fisk
told: second person
--
Fisk makes his mark and goes back to the back bench, and the men there make room for him without looking at him.

[if garrard_ordered]
On the front bench, in a good coat, {garrard}, whom the village calls {garrard.aka}, crosses his legs the other way.

[end]
> [[Call Mr. Garrard->garrard]] ~ id call_garrard ~ become garrard ~ when garrard_ordered or gig_heard or light_on_bank
> [[The coroner sums up->summing_up]] ~ id sum_up ~ become clerk

:: garrard
told: first person
reveal garrard
--
@fenwick Your name, sir, for the record.

@garrard Edmund Garrard. Of the Grange. I sit on the Bench at Ely; Fenwick knows me perfectly well.

@fenwick The record does not.

They have heard a gig on the drove, and a lamp on the bank, and a shilling. I can feel the jury deciding whether to believe a gentleman. It is not, in my experience, a long decision.

> [[Say you dined alone and went to bed->garrard:dined]] ~ id dined ~ weighs truth ~ arc garrard holds ~ predict 80%
> [[Say you drove out to look at the water->garrard:drove]] ~ id drove ~ weighs duty ~ arc garrard falls ~ tone dark
> [[Suppose he had stayed at home->garrard:at_home]] ~ inquest for dined ~ id at_home

:: garrard:dined
set struck: yes
--
@garrard I dined alone and went up at ten. My man will say so.

@fenwick Your man is not here.

@garrard My man is not required to be.

[redacted until record_closed]
@garrard (rising) The Fen floods every winter, Fenwick. Eight hundred acres, and twelve families on them. A lock-keeper is paid to—

@fenwick Strike that. The clerk will strike that.

[end]
The clerk strikes it. The pen goes through the paper.

> [[Step down->summing_up]] ~ id garrard_down ~ become clerk

:: garrard:drove
set gig_heard: yes
set light_on_bank: yes
--
@garrard I drove out to look at the water. Anyone with land would have. I took the carriage lamp and stood on the bank above the lock for a quarter of an hour, and saw the level, and drove home.

@fenwick Did you see anyone at the sluice?

@garrard It was dark, Fenwick.

@fenwick You had a lamp.

> [[Step down->summing_up]] ~ id garrard_down2 ~ become clerk

:: garrard:at_home
(((
The second pending fork, with one home. MonkeyPaw tells the gentleman's
Suppose, to the brief `pride`; this is the fallback.
)))
pending: by monkeypaw ~ rejoin summing_up ~ brief pride ~ timeout 1m
--
@fenwick Suppose, Mr. Garrard, you had stayed at home that night with your dinner and your man. What would have become of your eight hundred acres?

@garrard (smiling) Under water, I expect, and twelve families on the parish. You’d have had a different inquest, Fenwick, and a longer one.

I say it to the jury, as a joke. Two of them laugh. The foreman writes something on his hat-band with a stub of pencil.

> [[Step down->summing_up]] ~ id garrard_down3 ~ become clerk

:: summing_up
told: second person
--
Mr. Fenwick turns his chair to the jury and puts the tips of his fingers together.

@fenwick Gentlemen. You have heard that the pins were {if pins_out then "drawn before ten" else "home at nine, and we do not know after"}. You have heard {if fisk_there then "that a man was at the sluice" else "of no one at the sluice but the deceased"}{if light_on_bank then ", and of a lamp on the bank" else ""}. You have heard the doctor.

@fenwick Your verdict must rest on what has been said in this room. Not on what you have heard in the Plough these five nights. On what has been said in this room.

You hand him the depositions. He weighs them in his hand as if the verdict were a matter of weight.

> [[The jury retires->verdict]] ~ id retire ~ beat climax ~ predict 60%

:: verdict [finale]
set record_closed: yes
--
The jury is out for forty minutes. When they come back the foreman stands, and looks at his hat, and then at Martha Rudd.

[if fisk_there and (fisk_confessed or a_blow)]
@foreman Unlawful killing, sir. By Edward Fisk.

[if fisk_there and (fisk_confessed or a_blow) and garrard_ordered]
@foreman And we wish to add, sir, that Mr. Garrard of the Grange ought to answer for the shilling.

[if amos_at_paddles and not fisk_there]
@foreman Death by misadventure, sir. He went to the sluice in the dark to do his duty, and the water had him.

[if not amos_at_paddles and not fisk_there]
@foreman Accidental death, sir. He fell.

[if fisk_there and not (fisk_confessed or a_blow)]
@foreman An open verdict, sir. We don’t know. We know there was a man there. We don’t know what he did.

[end]
> [[After->epilogue_unlawful]] ~ id after_unlawful ~ when fisk_there and (fisk_confessed or a_blow)
> [[After->epilogue_misadventure]] ~ id after_misadventure ~ when amos_at_paddles and not fisk_there
> [[After->epilogue_accident]] ~ id after_accident ~ when not amos_at_paddles and not fisk_there
> [[After->epilogue_open]] ~ id after_open ~ when fisk_there and not (fisk_confessed or a_blow)

:: epilogue_unlawful [epilogue]
--
Fisk is tried at the Cambridge Assizes in the spring for manslaughter, and gets five years.

[if garrard_ordered]
Mr. Garrard is not tried for anything. He resigns from the Bench at Ely, for his health, and in the summer the Grange is let.

[end]
In March they give Tom the keeping of Hythe Lock. He checks the gates four times a night, in order, and nobody says anything about it, and the lock is never once drawn without a keeper’s hand on the chain.

> [[The record->the_record]] ~ id record_unlawful

:: epilogue_misadventure [epilogue]
--
The *Ely Gazette* calls Amos Rudd a faithful servant of the Fen. The Grange sends a wreath.

Martha keeps the lantern on its hook. Some nights Tom takes it down, and goes out to the gates, and comes back, and takes it down again.

> [[The record->the_record]] ~ id record_misadventure

:: epilogue_accident [epilogue]
--
*Accidental death.* It is the shortest thing in your depositions book, and it took the longest to write.

Ned Fisk sells eels at Ely market all that winter. Nobody buys them from him twice.

> [[The record->the_record]] ~ id record_accident

:: epilogue_open [epilogue]
--
An open verdict leaves the door open. Nobody walks through it.

In the spring the Grange water runs down the lock cut every winter, and the new keeper, a stranger from Lincolnshire, draws the sluice whenever he is asked.

> [[The record->the_record]] ~ id record_open

:: the_record [epilogue]
(((
The last epilogue, after any of the others: the record, closed. What was
struck is shown now, because `record_closed` lifted the redaction.
)))
--
That night you copy the depositions fair, for the Clerk of the Peace at Ely.

[if struck]
You come to the gentleman’s, and find, under your own line through it, the words you were told to strike. You can read them perfectly well.

[quote letter ~ by "E. Garrard, struck from the record"]
The Fen floods every winter, Fenwick. Eight hundred acres, and twelve families on them. A lock-keeper is paid to—
[end]

You leave the line where it is.

[end]

The record holds what was said in the room. You wrote it down. All of it.

